"JavaScript registers declarations before running the code. Function declarations can be called before their line. `var` variables exist but hold `undefined`. `let` and `const` cannot be used before they are declared; that is the temporal dead zone and it throws an error."

```js
saludar(); // funciona: la declaración sube completa
function saludar() { return "hola"; }

let error = null;
try { edad; } catch (e) { error = e.name; } // ReferenceError
let edad = 20;
```

"A closure is a function that remembers the variables from where it was created, even after the outer function has finished. It is useful for private state. In React I use them constantly without noticing: an event handler remembers the state of the render in which it was created."

```js
function crearContador() {
  let cuenta = 0; // privada
  return () => ++cuenta;
}
const siguiente = crearContador();
siguiente(); // 1
siguiente(); // 2
```

"There are eight: `false`, `0`, `-0`, `0n`, the empty string, `null`, `undefined` and `NaN`. Everything else is truthy, including empty arrays and objects. Zero is the trap: `count || 10` replaces a valid zero with 10. That is what `??` is for, since it only reacts to `null` and `undefined`."

```js
const cantidad = 0;
const conOr = cantidad || 10;  // 10 (error típico)
const conNullish = cantidad ?? 10; // 0
```

"JavaScript runs one thing at a time. Asynchronous work, like a `fetch` or a timer, is handled by the browser, which queues the callback when done. The event loop checks: when the call stack is empty it first runs all microtasks, which are promises, and then one macrotask, such as a `setTimeout`. That is why a resolved promise always runs before a zero-delay timeout."

```js
const orden = [];
orden.push("A");
setTimeout(() => orden.push("D"), 0);
Promise.resolve().then(() => orden.push("C"));
orden.push("B");
// al final: A, B, C, D
```

"They are the same underneath. `async/await` reads like synchronous code, so I prefer it. Errors go in `try/catch`. One important detail: `fetch` does not reject on a 404 or 500, so I check `response.ok` myself."

```js
async function obtenerNoticias() {
  try {
    const respuesta = await fetch("/api/noticias");
    if (!respuesta.ok) throw new Error(`Error ${respuesta.status}`);
    return await respuesta.json();
  } catch (error) {
    console.error("No se pudieron cargar las noticias:", error.message);
    return [];
  }
}
```

"It means not changing existing data but creating a copy with the change. It matters because React detects changes by comparing references: if I mutate the same array with `push`, the reference stays the same and React does not notice. So I use spread, `map` and `filter`."

```js
const tareas = [{ id: 1, hecha: false }];
const conNueva = [...tareas, { id: 2, hecha: false }];
const actualizada = tareas.map((t) => (t.id === 1 ? { ...t, hecha: true } : t));
const sinPrimera = tareas.filter((t) => t.id !== 1);
```

"Spread copies only the first level: nested objects are still shared, so changing a nested one changes both. For a full copy I use `structuredClone`. In React I usually do not copy everything, only the branch that changes."

```js
const original = { nombre: "Ana", direccion: { ciudad: "Ica" } };
const superficial = { ...original };
superficial.direccion.ciudad = "Lima"; // también cambia en original
const profunda = structuredClone(original);
profunda.direccion.ciudad = "Cusco";   // original no cambia
```

"Destructuring pulls values out of an object or array into variables in one line. The three dots do two things depending on position: as spread they expand items, for example to copy; as rest they collect whatever is left into one variable."

```js
const usuario = { id: 7, nombre: "Angel", clave: "secreta" };
const { clave, ...publico } = usuario; // publico = { id: 7, nombre: "Angel" }
const [primero, ...resto] = [10, 20, 30]; // primero = 10, resto = [20, 30]
```

"Both limit how often a function runs. Debounce waits until the user stops: I use it in a search box so I do not fetch on every keystroke. Throttle runs at most once per interval: I use it for scroll events."

```js
function debounce(fn, ms) {
  let temporizador;
  return (...args) => {
    clearTimeout(temporizador);
    temporizador = setTimeout(() => fn(...args), ms);
  };
}
```

"Types that are checked before the code runs. It catches mistakes while I type, like passing a number where a string is expected or reading a property that does not exist. It also improves autocomplete and works as documentation. In React I use it to type props and API responses."

```ts
type Noticia = { id: string; titulo: string; fecha: string; destacada?: boolean };

function TarjetaNoticia({ noticia }: { noticia: Noticia }) {
  return <h2>{noticia.titulo}</h2>;
}
```

"For logic I write unit tests with Vitest. For components I use React Testing Library and test as a user would: find a button by its text, click it and check what shows on screen, without relying on internals. On an AWS project with my brothers I also set up tests in CI so they run on every change."

```jsx
test("suma uno al hacer clic", async () => {
  render(<Contador />);
  await userEvent.click(screen.getByRole("button", { name: "Sumar" }));
  expect(screen.getByText("Total: 1")).toBeInTheDocument();
});
```
